    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d419">

                <pb facs="0550" n="465" sameAs="frus:frus1919v02/pg465"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.51/897a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Davis</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-01-17T15:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 17,
                                1919, 3 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>4112. Following is substance of memorandum handed to British Chargé yesterday:
                        <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>Department has given careful consideration to suggestion British
                            Government that financial aid and rehabilitation of Liberia be
                            considered at the Peace Conference.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d419-f1"/>
                            American Peace Mission is of opinion that this subject has no bearing on
                            other questions for consideration at conference and should be settled
                            directly among the governments. Liberian question is not one affecting
                            West Africa. Liberia is an independent nation and should not be
                            considered in relation with captured German colonies in West Africa.
                            United States as Liberia’s next friend has always been looked to for
                            establishment necessary reforms and performance of obligations under
                            Loan Agreement.<anchor n="6" xml:id="d419-f2"/> This has been difficult
                            under financial <hi rendition="#italic">entente</hi> and Liberia has not
                            accomplished many things desired by Receivers. The time has now come
                            when United States desires to give favorable consideration to appeals of
                            Liberia and colored people in the United States. American Government
                            does not anticipate British Government will interpose objection to new
                            proposal as outlined in Department’s memorandum of November 19<anchor
                                n="7" xml:id="d419-f3"/> regarding all American Receivership. New
                            proposal in no way to be regarded as indicating slightest desire to
                            establish protectorate by United States but merely to maintain historic
                            position as Liberia’s next friend. This Government is particularly
                            anxious that opportunity for trade and investment be afforded foreign
                            capital subject to proper safeguards.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Polk</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
